# Vulnerability details

## Impact
To mitigate the issue of looping over potentially unbounded arrays, we need to ensure that loops in functions _calculateStakingRewards and _evict have a reasonable maximum iteration count. If necessary, break down large loops into multiple transactions.

## Proof of Concept
By introducing startIndex and endIndex parameters and enforcing a reasonable maximum iteration count, we can prevent gas limit issues when looping over potentially unbounded arrays. This approach ensures that loops in functions _calculateStakingRewards and _evict do not exceed the gas limit


### _calculateStakingRewards Function
Original _calculateStakingRewards Function:

function _calculateStakingRewards() internal view returns (
    uint256 lastAvailableRewards,
    uint256 numServices,
    uint256 totalRewards,
    uint256[] memory eligibleServiceIds,
    uint256[] memory eligibleServiceRewards,
    uint256[] memory serviceIds,
    uint256[][] memory serviceNonces,
    uint256[] memory serviceInactivity
) {
    uint256 tsCheckpointLast = tsCheckpoint;
    lastAvailableRewards = availableRewards;
    if (block.timestamp - tsCheckpointLast >= livenessPeriod && lastAvailableRewards > 0) {
        uint256 size = setServiceIds.length;

        serviceIds = new uint256[](size);
        eligibleServiceIds = new uint256[](size);
        eligibleServiceRewards = new uint256[](size);
        serviceNonces = new uint256[][](size);
        serviceInactivity = new uint256[](size);

        for (uint256 i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
            serviceIds[i] = setServiceIds[i];
            ServiceInfo storage sInfo = mapServiceInfo[serviceIds[i]];

            uint256 serviceCheckpoint = tsCheckpointLast;
            uint256 ts = sInfo.tsStart;
            if (ts > serviceCheckpoint) {
                serviceCheckpoint = ts;
            }

            ts = block.timestamp - serviceCheckpoint;
            bool ratioPass;
            (ratioPass, serviceNonces[i]) = _checkRatioPass(sInfo.multisig, sInfo.nonces, ts);

            if (ratioPass) {
                eligibleServiceRewards[numServices] = rewardsPerSecond * ts;
                totalRewards += eligibleServiceRewards[numServices];
                eligibleServiceIds[numServices] = serviceIds[i];
                ++numServices;
            } else {
                serviceInactivity[i] = ts;
            }
        }
    }
}

#### Updated _calculateStakingRewards Function with Maximum Iteration Count:

uint256 public constant MAX_ITERATIONS = 100;

function _calculateStakingRewards(uint256 startIndex, uint256 endIndex) internal view returns (
    uint256 lastAvailableRewards,
    uint256 numServices,
    uint256 totalRewards,
    uint256[] memory eligibleServiceIds,
    uint256[] memory eligibleServiceRewards,
    uint256[] memory serviceIds,
    uint256[][] memory serviceNonces,
    uint256[] memory serviceInactivity
) {
    uint256 tsCheckpointLast = tsCheckpoint;
    lastAvailableRewards = availableRewards;
    if (block.timestamp - tsCheckpointLast >= livenessPeriod && lastAvailableRewards > 0) {
        uint256 size = setServiceIds.length;
        require(endIndex <= size, "End index out of bounds");

        serviceIds = new uint256[](endIndex - startIndex);
        eligibleServiceIds = new uint256[](endIndex - startIndex);
        eligibleServiceRewards = new uint256[](endIndex - startIndex);
        serviceNonces = new uint256[][](endIndex - startIndex);
        serviceInactivity = new uint256[](endIndex - startIndex);

        for (uint256 i = startIndex; i < endIndex; ++i) {
            uint256 index = i - startIndex;
            serviceIds[index] = setServiceIds[i];
            ServiceInfo storage sInfo = mapServiceInfo[serviceIds[index]];

            uint256 serviceCheckpoint = tsCheckpointLast;
            uint256 ts = sInfo.tsStart;
            if (ts > serviceCheckpoint) {
                serviceCheckpoint = ts;
            }

            ts = block.timestamp - serviceCheckpoint;
            bool ratioPass;
            (ratioPass, serviceNonces[index]) = _checkRatioPass(sInfo.multisig, sInfo.nonces, ts);

            if (ratioPass) {
                eligibleServiceRewards[numServices] = rewardsPerSecond * ts;
                totalRewards += eligibleServiceRewards[numServices];
                eligibleServiceIds[numServices] = serviceIds[index];
                ++numServices;
            } else {
                serviceInactivity[index] = ts;
            }
        }
    }
}

### _evict Function
Original _evict Function:

function _evict(
    uint256[] memory evictServiceIds,
    uint256[] memory serviceInactivity,
    uint256 numEvictServices
) internal {
    uint256 totalNumServices = evictServiceIds.length;

    uint256[] memory serviceIds = new uint256[](numEvictServices);
    address[] memory owners = new address[](numEvictServices);
    address[] memory multisigs = new address[](numEvictServices);
    uint256[] memory inactivity = new uint256[](numEvictServices);
    uint256[] memory serviceIndexes = new uint256[](numEvictServices);

    uint256 sCounter;
    uint256 serviceId;
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < totalNumServices; ++i) {
        if (evictServiceIds[i] > 0) {
            serviceId = evictServiceIds[i];
            serviceIds[sCounter] = serviceId;

            ServiceInfo storage sInfo = mapServiceInfo[serviceId];
            owners[sCounter] = sInfo.owner;
            multisigs[sCounter] = sInfo.multisig;
            inactivity[sCounter] = serviceInactivity[i];
            serviceIndexes[sCounter] = i;
            sCounter++;
        }
    }

    for (uint256 i = numEvictServices; i > 0; --i) {
        totalNumServices--;
        uint256 idx = serviceIndexes[i - 1];
        setServiceIds[idx] = setServiceIds[totalNumServices];
        setServiceIds.pop();
    }

    emit ServicesEvicted(epochCounter, serviceIds, owners, multisigs, inactivity);
}

#### Updated _evict Function with Maximum Iteration Count:

function _evict(
    uint256[] memory evictServiceIds,
    uint256[] memory serviceInactivity,
    uint256 numEvictServices,
    uint256 startIndex,
    uint256 endIndex
) internal {
    require(endIndex <= evictServiceIds.length, "End index out of bounds");

    uint256[] memory serviceIds = new uint256[](numEvictServices);
    address[] memory owners = new address[](numEvictServices);
    address[] memory multisigs = new address[](numEvictServices);
    uint256[] memory inactivity = new uint256[](numEvictServices);
    uint256[] memory serviceIndexes = new uint256[](numEvictServices);

    uint256 sCounter;
    uint256 serviceId;
    for (uint256 i = startIndex; i < endIndex; ++i) {
        if (evictServiceIds[i] > 0) {
            serviceId = evictServiceIds[i];
            serviceIds[sCounter] = serviceId;

            ServiceInfo storage sInfo = mapServiceInfo[serviceId];
            owners[sCounter] = sInfo.owner;
            multisigs[sCounter] = sInfo.multisig;
            inactivity[sCounter] = serviceInactivity[i];
            serviceIndexes[sCounter] = i;
            sCounter++;
        }
    }

    for (uint256 i = numEvictServices; i > 0; --i) {
        uint256 idx = serviceIndexes[i - 1];
        setServiceIds[idx] = setServiceIds[setServiceIds.length - 1];
        setServiceIds.pop();
    }

    emit ServicesEvicted(epochCounter, serviceIds, owners, multisigs, inactivity);
}

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
1. Breaking Down the Large Loops
To break down the large loops, we can split the looping operations across multiple transactions. This approach typically involves maintaining state between calls and ensuring that a full loop can be broken into smaller parts. We introduce startIndex and endIndex to define the range of indices that each function call will process.

Example Usage
1. To use the modified _calculateStakingRewards function:

// Example function to call _calculateStakingRewards in parts
function calculateStakingRewardsInParts(uint256 startIndex, uint256 endIndex) external view returns (
    uint256 lastAvailableRewards,
    uint256 numServices,
    uint256 totalRewards,
    uint256[] memory eligibleServiceIds,
    uint256[] memory eligibleServiceRewards,
    uint256[] memory serviceIds,
    uint256[][] memory serviceNonces,
    uint256[] memory serviceInactivity
) {
    return _calculateStakingRewards(startIndex, endIndex);
}


2. To use the modified _evict function:

// Example function to call _evict in parts
function evictInParts(
    uint256[] memory evictServiceIds,
    uint256[] memory serviceInactivity,
    uint256 numEvictServices,
    uint256 startIndex,
    uint256 endIndex
) external {
    _evict(evictServiceIds, serviceInactivity, numEvictServices, startIndex, endIndex);
}


Conclusion
By introducing startIndex and endIndex parameters and enforcing a reasonable maximum iteration count, we can prevent gas limit issues when looping over potentially unbounded arrays. This approach ensures that loops in functions _calculateStakingRewards and _evict do not exceed the gas limit, thus improving the contract's security and reliability.


