# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In stakingBase.sol, the service struct contains the following:
``` 
// Total number of agent instances
uint32 maxNumAgentInstances;
// Actual number of agent instances
uint32 numAgentInstances;
```
This represents the total number of agencies and the actual number of agencies. However, in stakingParams, the struct only contains:
``` 
// Number of agent instances in the service
uint256 numAgentInstances;
```
while staking, `numAgentInstances` in the service struct is not used as a reference to `numAgentInstances` in stakingParams. Instead, it uses `maxNumAgentInstances`:
```
if (numAgentInstances != service.maxNumAgentInstances) {
    revert WrongServiceConfiguration(serviceId);
}
```
leaving ``numAgentInstances`` uninitialized.

## Proof of Concept
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/3ce502ec8b475885b90668e617f3983cea3ae29f/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L49-L66

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/3ce502ec8b475885b90668e617f3983cea3ae29f/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L176-L207

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/3ce502ec8b475885b90668e617f3983cea3ae29f/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L742-L744
## Tools Used
Manual
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
if the aim is to make use of ``maxNumAgentInstances`` and ``numAgentInstances``, This issue can be resolved by adding `uint32 maxNumAgentInstances;` to stakingParams and updating the staking logic to:
```
if (numAgentInstances != service.numAgentInstances || maxNumAgentInstances != service.maxNumAgentInstances) {
    revert WrongServiceConfiguration(serviceId);
}
```
or the service struct can be updated by removing `maxNumAgentInstances`, and the staking logic can be updated to:
```
if (numAgentInstances != service.numAgentInstances ) {
    revert WrongServiceConfiguration(serviceId);
}
```



